On 04.03.2014 21:48, mainiacjoe wrote:
> [...]  In this run, I am trying > Archaeologists.  [...]

One of the difficult roles.

> Anyway, I am becoming reminded of one of the frustrations I had in earlier
> Nethack sessions.  The early and mid-game are fascinating puzzles and full
> of tension as you encounter enemies you cannot handle well.  But eventually
> you get the luckstone at the bottom of the Mines and you stop missing in
> combat.  That takes away a lot of the suspense. 

I never thought about having the luckstone being a turning point. My
own turning points are multifold and defined by other tools, primarily
getting the resistances; poison resistance, MR, reflection, unihorn,
a permanent engraving wand, BoH or sack, and most important, finding
an (unattended or coaligned) altar to get an artifact.

In my current game, for example, I could not go to mines end and had
for the most difficult part of the game, the early and mid game phase,
no luck, or (at some point) only temporaray luck from sacrifices. But
I am going as a rogue, and with lot of effective missiles maximum luck
seems not to be a primary issue. (I found a random luckstone later.)

> Once your XL is in the mid
> teens Luck + XL + monster AC are rarely less than 20, and this before
> taking weapon skill and enchantment into account.
> 
> I recently did the Archaeologist quest for the first time.  I had uncursed
> +4 Demonbane two-weaponed with an uncursed +0 long sword.  The quest
> nemesis did not get a single action. [...]

Yes, a lot of the success depends on the artifacts you have; the earlier
the better.

> It was by accident and not design
> that I woke him on the first of a two-action turn (intrinsic speed only)
> but still, Demonbane hits, 2nd sword hits, Demonbane hits, he dies.  I
> didn't know how tough he'd be and had some elaborate designs set up for a
> protracted fight including warps to the upstair, but they were all for
> naught.  To say this was anti-climactic would be an understatement.

Yes, I concur; some roles/races with [real life] luck to have got some
perfect fitting equipment may have an easy game at some point.

But in my experience those games are not "normal"; often I have to be
careful for a long time, travel forth and back, to get over that point
(or die). I can't speak for others here, but if you *try* to win every
game you feel that games are not guaranteed to evolve that smoothly.

There's another effect to consider as well; the better my game evolves
the less I seem to care preparing myself. So it may get tight in some
situations because I advance and descend too quickly. In the same line
I often don't play in an optimal way any more because I am confident
to be safe; which is what you describe subsequently...

> It is something of a let-down that from here on out, I feel like I am
> unlikely to encounter situations I cannot handle.  If I die, it is because
> I handled a tractable situation stupidly, such as described above. 

> I am
> not likely to need to run away from something over my head and prepare to
> come back.

This is what I often think to be the case - but sometimes I just missed
that I forgot to be prepared in one way or the other, still missing some
crucial resistance, not being able to handle a concourse or accumulation
of events, or similar.

> Now of course I've just jinxed my character, and granted I
> suspect that this statement reflects my inexperience, but at this point I'm
> feeling like the game would have more suspense if I would miss in combat
> every once in a while. 

Well, where many of my characters get killed a lot is missing in melee in
the early game, indeed; in those cases I often have the impression that
Nethack can be too hard in that game phase; if you don't find anything
appropriate, are blocked to advance deeper or retreat, and then miss the
critters, it's often a feeling of being helpless without any way to escape
and survive.

> Yet I recognize too that the game cannot be too hard for unspoiled players.

I'm very spoiled and quite experienced and nonetheless feel some games to
be just cursed. Not being able to hit monsters, while your pet got killed
at a falling rock trap on the upper levels, is one way.

Later it's often overconfidence, inattentiveness, or being underprepared
that kills me. Given those "external" effects I am quite satisfied with
the degree of challenge in the late game; it's not perfect but I think ok.
(As mentioned elsethread, there are variants that improved the late game
in this respect.)

> Am I the only one who feels this way?  Those here such as Janis who ascend
> frequently, what do you do to manage the tedium of so much game left after
> you become invincible in combat?

There's two issues here; tedium and being "invincible". The tediousness
I handle by shortcutting those parts; as a side effect the degree of my
preparedness and the amounts of items I find is reduced. Being invincible
leads often to overconfidence; if there's then some accumulation of bad
events I get in trouble or die. This issue can be handled by discipline,
but in my case it doesn't always work. ;-}

> PS I must say, "no luckitem" seems like a challenging voluntary conduct
> that I hadn't thought of until composing this post.

In my opinion it would be a minor one, similar to "no genocide". Mind that
you can compensate the lack of a luck-item easily by fostering temporary
luck if you have convenient altars available.

